<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William I, Elector of Hesse</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1743–<a href="/years/1821">1821</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as William IX of Hesse-Kassel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William I, Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, ruled Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a> as Landgrave William IX from 1785 and became an elector in 1803. His wealth came largely from hiring out soldiers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> drove him out in 1806 and gave his lands to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>, but he returned in 1813 and kept the electoral title after the Empire ended.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-i-elector-of-hesse.jpg" alt="William I, Elector of Hesse" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wilhelm Böttner · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wilhelm_IX._Landgraf_von_Hessel-Kassel_Boettner.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
